{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794551098360,"sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","part":{"id":"prt_1d394abf800154i7kBcnHipi53","sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","messageID":"msg_1d3949cd00010RYxg2OmGzZCCY","type":"step-start","snapshot":"5ad58c0c835dc48d3e8a367b41c01acb181f0636"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794551112044,"sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","part":{"partID":"prt_1d394dc47001eoET6UPLc79FV6","sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","messageID":"msg_1d3949cd00010RYxg2OmGzZCCY","type":"tool","id":"call_0ba79a7764c14a1fae692e49","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && for f in .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/README.md; do echo \"===FILE $f\"; grep -n \"parent-named\\|AI_SESSION_CHILD\\|child session\\|child\\b\\|mutating\\|policy\" \"$f\" | head -14; done"},"output":"===FILE .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\n25:The single most important property is that it **fails open**: the prompt always proceeds. When the advisor call times out, fails or finds no route, the handler and the OpenCode plugin emit the directives fallback instead of a brief. An unparseable payload, an unhandled error or a shim that cannot relay its child resolves to `{}`. Every adapter honors the `skill-advisor` kill-switch, default-on.\n38:4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. It also carries `compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, because each route lookup is a subprocess inside the hook's budget and the brief leads with the top pick. The hook keeps the `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. A daemon still running an older build rejects these options with JSON-RPC `-32602`, and the CLI retries once without them.\n39:5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. With `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`, a surfaced `start` or `ask` command, or a `run` command that is not read-only, renders as `(parent-named only)`. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n43:The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n50:<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\n53:The recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A recommendation with no command row keeps its skill label rather than a command label.\n75:- A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n88:| **Claude** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `system-skill-advisor/.../dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.claude/settings.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 3s | Shim resolves the advisor target by walking up to the `.opencode` ancestor (install-anchored, not CWD-relative). Bounded stdin (1 MiB), bounded child stdio, 2.5s child timeout, `SIGKILL` on timeout | `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON on stdout. `{}` on any failure |\n192:| Bounded | Shims bound stdin (1 MiB), child stdio (1 MiB), child timeout (2.5s), and `SIGKILL` on timeout. OpenCode bounds bridge stdout (256 KiB) and uses grace termination. Pi bounds captured user text (32 KiB, 64-session LRU). |\n===FILE .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md\n24:`hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` reads the prompt from stdin, calls the native advisor and returns a JSON envelope with `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. The hook honors `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` and fails open on any daemon-level failure. As a default-off, fail-open shadow it also records post-emission advisor-policy delivery (`observeEmittedAdvisorPolicy`), an observed receipt with `lifecycleEpoch >= 1`, as the final step before returning, leaving the emitted envelope byte-identical. Freshness vocabulary is `live / stale / absent / unavailable`. Status vocabulary is `ok / skipped / degraded / fail_open`.\n40:Hook diagnostics are prompt-safe rollups handled by `lib/metrics.ts`. The `spec_kit.<group>.<metric_name>` instrumentation namespace emits only when `SPECKIT_METRICS_ENABLED=true`. Default off is no emission and is byte-identical to the pre-instrumentation surface. Alert thresholds for the rolling cache-hit p95 are read from the environment with conservative defaults: `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_HOOK_CACHE_HIT_P95_WARN_MS` (default 75) and `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_HOOK_CACHE_HIT_P95_PAGE_MS` (default 150). Both flags are forwarded to the daemon child through the launcher `CHILD_ENV_ALLOWLIST` so the same thresholds reach every runtime.\n63:| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/tests/user-prompt-submit-shim.vitest.ts` | Automated test | shim child budget |\n===FILE .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/README.md\n21:| `skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts` | Resolves the CLI, IPC bridge and database paths from one root (`.skilled`, then `.opencode`), spawns the advisor CLI with a bounded timeout. It sends the request on stdin with `--json -`, so the prompt never appears in the child's argv. The request it sends carries `options.includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route, and `options.compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, so only the top pick gets a route lookup. `recommendationFromCli` keeps a non-empty `command` on each recommendation and reads nothing from the route itself. It runs the CLI with `--no-warm-only`, because the CLI is the component that starts the daemon on a cold socket. It parses its JSON payload and normalizes the result (and retryable exit/reason codes) into an `AdvisorHookResult`. |\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"===FILE .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\n25:The single most important property is that it **fails open**: the prompt always proceeds. When the advisor call times out, fails or finds no route, the handler and the OpenCode plugin emit the directives fallback instead of a brief. An unparseable payload, an unhandled error or a shim that cannot relay its child resolves to `{}`. Every adapter honors the `skill-advisor` kill-switch, default-on.\n38:4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. It also carries `compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, because each route lookup is a subprocess inside the hook's budget and the brief leads with the top pick. The hook keeps the `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. A daemon still running an older build rejects these options with JSON-RPC `-32602`, and the CLI retries once without them.\n39:5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. With `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`, a surfaced `start` or `ask` command, or a `run` command that is not read-only, renders as `(parent-named only)`. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n43:The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n50:<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\n53:The recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A recommendation with no command row keeps its skill label rather than a command label.\n75:- A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n88:| **Claude** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `system-skill-advisor/.../dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.claude/settings.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 3s | Shim resolves the advisor target by walking up to the `.opencode` ancestor (install-anchored, not CWD-relative). Bounded stdin (1 MiB), bounded child stdio, 2.5s child timeout, `SIGKILL` on timeout | `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON on stdout. `{}` on any failure |\n192:| Bounded | Shims bound stdin (1 MiB), child stdio (1 MiB), child timeout (2.5s), and `SIGKILL` on timeout. OpenCode bounds bridge stdout (256 KiB) and uses grace termination. Pi bounds captured user text (32 KiB, 64-session LRU). |\n===FILE .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md\n24:`hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` reads the prompt from stdin, calls the native advisor and returns a JSON envelope with `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. The hook honors `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` and fails open on any daemon-level failure. As a default-off, fail-open shadow it also records post-emission advisor-policy delivery (`observeEmittedAdvisorPolicy`), an observed receipt with `lifecycleEpoch >= 1`, as the final step before returning, leaving the emitted envelope byte-identical. Freshness vocabulary is `live / stale / absent / unavailable`. Status vocabulary is `ok / skipped / degraded / fail_open`.\n40:Hook diagnostics are prompt-safe rollups handled by `lib/metrics.ts`. The `spec_kit.<group>.<metric_name>` instrumentation namespace emits only when `SPECKIT_METRICS_ENABLED=true`. Default off is no emission and is byte-identical to the pre-instrumentation surface. Alert thresholds for the rolling cache-hit p95 are read from the environment with conservative defaults: `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_HOOK_CACHE_HIT_P95_WARN_MS` (default 75) and `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_HOOK_CACHE_HIT_P95_PAGE_MS` (default 150). Both flags are forwarded to the daemon child through the launcher `CHILD_ENV_ALLOWLIST` so the same thresholds reach every runtime.\n63:| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/tests/user-prompt-submit-shim.vitest.ts` | Automated test | shim child budget |\n===FILE .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/README.md\n21:| `skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts` | Resolves the CLI, IPC bridge and database paths from one root (`.skilled`, then `.opencode`), spawns the advisor CLI with a bounded timeout. It sends the request on stdin with `--json -`, so the prompt never appears in the child's argv. The request it sends carries `options.includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route, and `options.compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, so only the top pick gets a route lookup. `recommendationFromCli` keeps a non-empty `command` on each recommendation and reads nothing from the route itself. It runs the CLI with `--no-warm-only`, because the CLI is the component that starts the daemon on a cold socket. It parses its JSON payload and normalizes the result (and retryable exit/reason codes) into an `AdvisorHookResult`. |\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && for f in .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/README.md; do echo \\\"===FILE $f\\\"; grep -n \\\"parent-named\\\\|AI_SESSION_CHILD\\\\|child session\\\\|child\\\\b\\\\|mutating\\\\|policy\\\" \\\"$f\\\" | head -14; done\"}"},"time":{"start":1794551110727,"end":1794551112044}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794551114459,"sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","part":{"id":"prt_1d394eadb001UNh8jl22x5eSz7","sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","messageID":"msg_1d3949cd00010RYxg2OmGzZCCY","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"117b98594aac24f34dcf4efdd183d868aacf99f3","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":3542,"output":168,"reasoning":1204,"cache":{"read":176896,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794551121513,"sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","part":{"id":"prt_1d3950669001Cn74b9P2U7yUoP","sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","messageID":"msg_1d394eaf0001xuKlWpJZC9cWvs","type":"step-start","snapshot":"122665fd54267e9c1d5e7fee0df00865fd459774"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794551141123,"sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3954ea80016sZydve8XBRyVo","sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","messageID":"msg_1d394eaf0001xuKlWpJZC9cWvs","type":"tool","id":"call_92bed05d0b8c483bbe970462","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && grep -n \"policy\\|mutating\\|command\\b\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md | head -18; echo \"=== CLI surface ===\"; grep -n \"policy\\|mutating\\|holdout\\|child\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/skill-advisor-cli.md | head -18"},"output":"3:description: \"Native command that returns prompt-safe skill recommendations with lane attribution, lifecycle metadata, fail-open freshness semantics and an optional compiled-routing enrichment.\"\n17:Expose the native scoring pipeline as a command that any runtime can call through the CLI front door, with prompt-safe attribution and lifecycle-aware redirects.\n23:`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` implements the command. Input is validated by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` (Zod strict). The public response always includes the resolved `workspaceRoot` plus `effectiveThresholds`, where `effectiveThresholds` publishes the active `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold` and `confidenceOnly` mode after request overrides are merged with defaults. Output also carries prompt-safe `recommendations[]`, optional `laneBreakdown[]` when `includeAttribution: true`, lifecycle redirect metadata (`redirectFrom`, `redirectTo`, `status`), `freshness`, `trustState`, `generatedAt`, `cache`, optional `warnings` and optional `abstainReasons`. Fail-open states such as disabled or absent freshness still preserve `workspaceRoot` and `effectiveThresholds` in the envelope so callers can inspect the resolved repository scope and active routing thresholds even when no recommendations are returned. The handler enforces sanitization at the envelope boundary (see `lib/derived/sanitizer.ts`) and never echoes prompt text into response metadata. Cache behavior is tied to generation (see [`daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md`](../../feature-catalog/daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md)).\n33:When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:310`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must also contain `/deep:` or a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command.\n35:The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:563-565`, `632-634`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:287`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n37:The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute` or `command`. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:290`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n39:A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\n41:### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n43:Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n45:`lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `COMMAND_POLICIES[command]` because that generated table is keyed by runnable string, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n47:The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n60:| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Shared | `attachCommand()`, the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table and the `DEPTH_WORDING` gate that name the slash command for a single routed mode. |\n69:| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. `compiledRouteTopOnly: true` looks up the first recommendation alone. A command that ranks through its own row is not offered again by its hub. |\n=== CLI surface ===\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3:description: \"Native command that returns prompt-safe skill recommendations with lane attribution, lifecycle metadata, fail-open freshness semantics and an optional compiled-routing enrichment.\"\n17:Expose the native scoring pipeline as a command that any runtime can call through the CLI front door, with prompt-safe attribution and lifecycle-aware redirects.\n23:`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` implements the command. Input is validated by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` (Zod strict). The public response always includes the resolved `workspaceRoot` plus `effectiveThresholds`, where `effectiveThresholds` publishes the active `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold` and `confidenceOnly` mode after request overrides are merged with defaults. Output also carries prompt-safe `recommendations[]`, optional `laneBreakdown[]` when `includeAttribution: true`, lifecycle redirect metadata (`redirectFrom`, `redirectTo`, `status`), `freshness`, `trustState`, `generatedAt`, `cache`, optional `warnings` and optional `abstainReasons`. Fail-open states such as disabled or absent freshness still preserve `workspaceRoot` and `effectiveThresholds` in the envelope so callers can inspect the resolved repository scope and active routing thresholds even when no recommendations are returned. The handler enforces sanitization at the envelope boundary (see `lib/derived/sanitizer.ts`) and never echoes prompt text into response metadata. Cache behavior is tied to generation (see [`daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md`](../../feature-catalog/daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md)).\n33:When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:310`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must also contain `/deep:` or a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command.\n35:The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:563-565`, `632-634`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:287`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n37:The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute` or `command`. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:290`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n39:A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\n41:### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n43:Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n45:`lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `COMMAND_POLICIES[command]` because that generated table is keyed by runnable string, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n47:The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n60:| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Shared | `attachCommand()`, the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table and the `DEPTH_WORDING` gate that name the slash command for a single routed mode. |\n69:| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. `compiledRouteTopOnly: true` looks up the first recommendation alone. A command that ranks through its own row is not offered again by its hub. |\n=== CLI surface ===\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && grep -n \\\"policy\\\\|mutating\\\\|command\\\\b\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md | head -18; echo \\\"=== CLI surface ===\\\"; grep -n \\\"policy\\\\|mutating\\\\|holdout\\\\|child\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/skill-advisor-cli.md | head -18\"}"},"time":{"start":1794551140008,"end":1794551141123}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794551153029,"sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","part":{"partID":"prt_1d39547ba0017522X9RHBqfg2B","sessionID":"ses_e2c799690ffeJQrMJ5OzboPGaV","messageID":"msg_1d394eaf0001xuKlWpJZC9cWvs","type":"tool","id":"call_54c0d93118754b5595d5bf89","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && grep -n \"PARENT_NAMED_POLICY\\s*=\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts; echo \"=== claude timeout var ===\"; grep -rn \"SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor --include=*.ts --include=*.md | head -5; echo \"=== opencode timeout var ===\"; grep -rn \"SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS\" .skilled .opencode --include=*.ts --include=*.js --include=*.md --include=*.yaml 2>/dev/null | head -8"},"output":"113:const PARENT_NAMED_POLICY = 'parent-named only';\n=== claude timeout var ===\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md:36:`SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` bounds the native advisor subprocess and the remaining CLI fallback window. The CLI budget for the hook defaults to 2500 ms. The Claude shim in system-spec-kit sets it for the hook it spawns to the operator's value capped at 2200 ms. A value that is unset or does not parse to a positive integer becomes 2200 ms. The shim kills the hook at 2500 ms, so the cap leaves the hook time to print its fallback before the kill.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/pi-prompt-advisor.md:26:The in-process call races a timer set to the advisor budget plus `PI_ADVISOR_DEADLINE_MARGIN_MS` (300 ms). The budget is `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` or 2500 ms when that variable holds no number. A call that hangs past the deadline delivers `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective()` output instead of the advisor context. The deadline path passes the shared `SETUP_WORDING` check to the fallback renderer, so matching prompts show the same `/doctor:setup` hint.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:142:The advisor ships prompt-submit adapters for Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin and Pi, plus the OpenCode plugin at `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`. The native adapters and Pi run the same hook handler, which gates the prompt, calls the advisor CLI and renders with `renderAdvisorBrief`; the OpenCode plugin calls the CLI itself. When the handler has no brief, including after a timeout or a no-route result, it emits the directives fallback and lets the prompt proceed. The Claude adapter applies `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` to its advisor subprocess. When the operator leaves it unset, the Claude shim sets 2,200 ms for the hook under its own 2,500 ms kill so the fallback prints in time. Pi runs the handler in-process and races it against the budget plus 300 ms. The hook's request sends `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. The hook keeps that `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. The OpenCode plugin does not send the option and gets the default of true. The plugin appends the brief to the system prompt and exposes the advisor status tool.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/prompt-advisor.vitest.ts:48:const ORIGINAL_TIMEOUT = process.env.SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS;\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/prompt-advisor.vitest.ts:58:      delete process.env.SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS;\n=== opencode timeout var ===\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/config/hook-system.md:130:The timeout flag's ownership is the `system-skill-advisor` hub because its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts` and `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py`. See the advisor hub's runtime contract for `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` and its default `3000` ms. This sibling reference is a pointer, not the owner of that flag.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/ENV-REFERENCE.md:170:trio and `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS`. Those controls survived the memory decommission.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/ENV-REFERENCE.md:192:| `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` | `3000` | number | Owned by the skill-advisor hub for the OpenCode plugin bridge and advisor subprocesses. On timeout the bridge returns a prompt-safe degraded advisory brief instead of empty output. Set before launching OpenCode. | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/subprocess.ts` |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/runtime/lib/subprocess.js:29:    return parsePositiveInt(process.env.SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS, DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS);\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/subprocess.ts:119:  return parsePositiveInt(process.env.SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS, DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS);\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:97:`SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` is owned by this hub because its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts` and `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py`. Its default is `3000` ms. A timeout yields prompt-safe degraded context with a timeout marker. The system-spec-kit environment reference points here rather than defining a second contract.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md:176:Additional controls: `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_FORCE_LOCAL=1` (force local Python fallback where supported) and `--force-native` / `--force-local` / `--threshold` / `--stdin` (Python shim flags, default threshold `0.8`). `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 3000) is owned by the `system-skill-advisor` hub: its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts`, `skill-advisor-brief.ts`, the OpenCode bridge and `skill_advisor.py`. On timeout the OpenCode bridge serves prompt-safe stale context with a timeout marker. OpenCode tuning: `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CACHE_TTL_MS` (default 5 min), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_TOKENS` (default 80), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_BRIEF_CHARS` (default 2 KiB), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_PROMPT_BYTES` (default 64 KiB, measured on the whole stdin request after JSON escaping), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_COMPILED_ROUTE_BOUNDING` (compiled-route summary bounding).\n.opencode/plugins/README.md:177:Optional tuning: `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 3000, owned by `system-skill-advisor`), `SYSTEM_OPENCODE_TRANSFORM_DEDUP=1` (opt-in transform dedup for the advisor plugin), plus per-plugin cache/budget/timeout envs documented in each plugin's README.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"113:const PARENT_NAMED_POLICY = 'parent-named only';\n=== claude timeout var ===\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md:36:`SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` bounds the native advisor subprocess and the remaining CLI fallback window. The CLI budget for the hook defaults to 2500 ms. The Claude shim in system-spec-kit sets it for the hook it spawns to the operator's value capped at 2200 ms. A value that is unset or does not parse to a positive integer becomes 2200 ms. The shim kills the hook at 2500 ms, so the cap leaves the hook time to print its fallback before the kill.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/pi-prompt-advisor.md:26:The in-process call races a timer set to the advisor budget plus `PI_ADVISOR_DEADLINE_MARGIN_MS` (300 ms). The budget is `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` or 2500 ms when that variable holds no number. A call that hangs past the deadline delivers `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective()` output instead of the advisor context. The deadline path passes the shared `SETUP_WORDING` check to the fallback renderer, so matching prompts show the same `/doctor:setup` hint.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:142:The advisor ships prompt-submit adapters for Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin and Pi, plus the OpenCode plugin at `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`. The native adapters and Pi run the same hook handler, which gates the prompt, calls the advisor CLI and renders with `renderAdvisorBrief`; the OpenCode plugin calls the CLI itself. When the handler has no brief, including after a timeout or a no-route result, it emits the directives fallback and lets the prompt proceed. The Claude adapter applies `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` to its advisor subprocess. When the operator leaves it unset, the Claude shim sets 2,200 ms for the hook under its own 2,500 ms kill so the fallback prints in time. Pi runs the handler in-process and races it against the budget plus 300 ms. The hook's request sends `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. The hook keeps that `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. The OpenCode plugin does not send the option and gets the default of true. The plugin appends the brief to the system prompt and exposes the advisor status tool.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/prompt-advisor.vitest.ts:48:const ORIGINAL_TIMEOUT = process.env.SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS;\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/prompt-advisor.vitest.ts:58:      delete process.env.SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS;\n=== opencode timeout var ===\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/config/hook-system.md:130:The timeout flag's ownership is the `system-skill-advisor` hub because its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts` and `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py`. See the advisor hub's runtime contract for `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` and its default `3000` ms. This sibling reference is a pointer, not the owner of that flag.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/ENV-REFERENCE.md:170:trio and `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS`. Those controls survived the memory decommission.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/EN